    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>African socialism holds that traditional African cultures and community structures lean naturally toward socialism, so that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> needed no class struggle and its socialism differs from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism">Marxism</a> and European socialist theory.</li><li>Several African countries adopted socialist systems from the 1950s to the mid-1980s, about 35 of the 50 to 53 countries of the time, and Ghana, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> and Tanzania took it as a model of economic development.</li><li>Tanzania's Ujamaa villagisation of 1968 to 1975 fell short of its goals, which the general international consensus attributes to its accelerated timeline and bureaucratic inefficiency, though by the end of Nyerere's term 96% of children had gone to primary school.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Senghor answered a question about nationalising French companies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> by saying that it would be to &quot;kill the goose that laid the golden egg&quot;, and Senegal kept the French franc as its currency.</p></aside>
    </section>

